WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:22.330
LEIGH: Well, good morning, ladies and gentlemen,
and welcome to this meeting, which the Council

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:29.900
on Foreign Relations has organized in a very
timely way, which will be focusing on developments

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:37.309
in what in Brussels we have to refer to as
the country that I know best, the United Kingdom,

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:43.309
and its relations with the European Union
in light of the upcoming referendum, in just

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:49.299
eight days from now, on whether the United
Kingdom will remain as a member of the European

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:51.480
Union or whether it will leave.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.909
Just let me take a couple of minutes to say
what this means to me, both politically, but

00:00:57.909 --> 00:01:01.860
also personally when I think back on the last
few decades.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:11.910
I joined the European institutions in Brussels
in the late 1970s and had a brief pause from

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:18.320
my academic career of some 34 years in the
European institutions, during which I saw

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:28.530
them transformed utterly from a rather inward-looking
and totally Francophone club when I joined

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:37.070
to a far more global, open, and transparent
set of institutions when I left five years

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:39.430
ago to-
STELZENM�LLER: And Anglophone.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:40.510
LEIGH: Sorry?

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:41.510
STELZENM�LLER: And mostly Anglophone these
days.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:42.880
LEIGH: And predominantly Anglophone.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:50.390
I remember the key moment when this switch
occurred, when a very senior French official

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.950
had difficulties because he was presiding
a meeting and in front of him, on the name

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:04.260
plaque, there was "president" without an accent
on the E. And this created a diplomatic incident,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.739
whereas two or three years later this was
something that no one thought twice about.

00:02:08.739 --> 00:02:13.510
And certainly enlargement, that I was involved
with for much of my career-seeing, first of

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:19.191
all, Greece, Spain, and Portugal, after the
overthrow of their dictatorships, join the

00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:30.080
EU; later on, Sweden, Finland, and Austria;
and later still the countries of Central and

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.810
Eastern Europe, Malta, and Cyprus-really transformed
what had been a rather inward-looking club

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:43.959
to an association of nations, of 28 nations,
very highly integrated, and working across

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:53.740
a range of political, functional, strategic,
diplomatic issues to further the interests

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.830
of the continent.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:03:03.320
And therefore, in personal terms, it was a
great shock to me to see the gathering momentum

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:10.360
in the United Kingdom over the last two or
three years in favor of withdrawal from the

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.489
European Union.

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:23.010
This began with a kind of movement within
the Conservative Party, which was then upended

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:27.760
by the creation or the further development
of a new party, the United Kingdom Independence

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.200
Party.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:36.690
And the response by the prime minister at
the time, Cameron, to this was not to forcefully

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:43.050
present the arguments why he considered that
remaining in the European Union was in the

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:48.870
interest of the United Kingdom-arguments that
he's putting forward today-but rather to throw,

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:56.280
as they put it, red meat to the Euroskeptics
by promising a referendum if he were elected

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.740
prime minister in the last general election.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:05.640
And this was an important part of his platform
that he thought would preempt, to some extent,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.750
the issue and put it on the back burner.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:13.150
But, in fact, it had exactly the contrary
effect.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:18.829
And although he entered into negotiation with
the authorities in the European Union, the

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:23.750
different member states, in order to get a
new deal for Britain that he said he would

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:31.300
present to the electorate as the basis for
the referendum-and he did obtain certain concessions,

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:36.099
which were much less far-reaching than he'd
originally demanded-but nonetheless, it's

00:04:36.099 --> 00:04:44.289
really only in the last six weeks or so that
he has begun to present in strong, positive

00:04:44.289 --> 00:04:52.080
terms the reason why he believes that EU membership
is conducive to British security, stability,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.599
economic growth, jobs, and so on.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:05:00.749
And some of us rather wonder, if he felt that
all along, why did he take the risk of offering

00:05:00.749 --> 00:05:01.969
a referendum?

00:05:01.969 --> 00:05:08.869
As things stand today, the prospects for the
referendum next week are, in a way, anybody's

00:05:08.869 --> 00:05:09.869
guess.

00:05:09.869 --> 00:05:11.969
The polls have been up and down.

00:05:11.969 --> 00:05:17.909
Those of you who are following this know that
in the last days it looks as though the leave

00:05:17.909 --> 00:05:24.469
campaign has a slight lead, varying lead according
to which polls you look at.

00:05:24.469 --> 00:05:27.199
But we'll come to that in a few moments.

00:05:27.199 --> 00:05:32.949
The only real judge will be the electorate
next week, when they go into the ballot box.

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:39.849
I thought until fairly recently that, despite
all the arguments-the arguments on the leave

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:47.369
side, which are very much based on emotion
and concerns about immigration-sounds familiar?-and

00:05:47.369 --> 00:05:54.189
on the remain side, arguments predominantly
based on the economy-I was reasonably confident,

00:05:54.189 --> 00:06:00.409
until a few days ago, that when British voters
go into the ballot box, and in the privacy

00:06:00.409 --> 00:06:06.490
of that moment when they have to decide, having
been presented with no clear vision of what

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:11.860
life would be like outside the European Union,
and having heard President Obama, President

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:18.339
Xi of China, the leaders of industry, investors
in the U.K., and others all saying that they

00:06:18.339 --> 00:06:22.550
were convinced it was in Britain's interest
to remain, and they would have to review their

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:29.580
own policies, investment decisions, and so
on if the U.K. left, I really was convinced

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:36.289
that in the last resort this would lead voters
to play it safe, and go for the status quo,

00:06:36.289 --> 00:06:37.449
and vote for remain.

00:06:37.449 --> 00:06:43.629
But in the last few days, this has been rather
called into question, and the leave campaign

00:06:43.629 --> 00:06:50.219
has sought to preempt some of this by presenting
its own sort of mini-vision of what would

00:06:50.219 --> 00:06:55.221
happen the day after and the months after
and the years after, if the U.K. decides to

00:06:55.221 --> 00:06:56.221
leave.

00:06:56.221 --> 00:07:04.819
So it really is a crucial moment for us, extremely
timely, and we have a very, very distinguished

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:11.330
and well-informed panel who will investigate
some of these issues and give you their views

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.439
before engaging in a discussion with you on
some of the issues that are on your mind,

00:07:15.439 --> 00:07:19.819
including, I think very importantly here in
Washington, what all this means for the United

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:26.759
States, and indeed why Americans should care
about the outcome of this situation.

00:07:26.759 --> 00:07:36.189
So let me begin by turning to Sebastian Mallaby
and asking whether the kind of predictions

00:07:36.189 --> 00:07:41.020
that have been made, that we've read about,
issued by the Bank of England, by the Treasury

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:49.090
in the U.K., by the OECD, the IMF, and others
as to the economic impact of Britain leaving

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:55.409
the European Union-Brexit, as it's come to
be known-are these convincing, in economic

00:07:55.409 --> 00:07:56.409
terms?

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:59.689
Is the situation as dire as it's presented?

00:07:59.689 --> 00:08:01.529
Might it be better, or perhaps even worse?

00:08:01.529 --> 00:08:03.740
How do you see these things, Sebastian?

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:08.069
MALLABY: Well, I think that, you know, there's
been, as you say, this series of blue-chip

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:15.089
forecasts-the IMF, the OECD, the Bank of England,
the Treasury, London School of Economics,

00:08:15.089 --> 00:08:19.990
and various others-and they are, without exception,
projecting both a near-term economic shock

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.759
on a longer-term growth cost.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:30.659
There's one study out there, if you call it
a study, which is produced by the leave camp,

00:08:30.659 --> 00:08:38.290
that pooh-poohs this and says Britain would
do better in a leave scenario.

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:43.790
That's based on what I call the Cato Institute
fallacy, to be a little bit rude about our

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:45.340
friends around here.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:50.030
The Cato Institute fallacy is that if you
leave, suddenly you will deregulate everything

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:55.200
in sight in a way that you would never have
done before, but suddenly you would kind of

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.680
get out of bed in the morning and you would
be shocked into this, you know, pattern of

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:03.970
having unilateral removal of all trade tariffs,
for example.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:10.700
So the vision presented to come up with this
positive projection is completely politically

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:11.700
implausible.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:17.680
What I think is much more plausible is that
the consensus projections of a shock at the

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.510
beginning and then some growth loss down the
road are, in fact, too optimistic.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:27.330
And I say that because uncertainty is very
hard to model, and the amount of political

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:31.770
uncertainty that we're going to get from a
leave is extreme.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:35.250
So the first thing that would happen is that
probably David Cameron, the prime minister,

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.800
who says he would try to stay in office, would
be forced out.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.960
Ken Clarke, the former finance minister and
kind of Conservative grand figure, said he

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.130
would last 30 seconds.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:49.140
That might be a little bit short, but I think
he would be gone.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.830
You'd then have a leadership contest in the
Conservative Party, which involves a two-round

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:57.070
stage where the parliamentary party choosing
two candidates, putting it to the members.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:58.810
Those members vote by mail.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:00.280
That takes three months.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:03.530
So for three months, you have a leadership
struggle going on.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:08.010
In the meantime, you'll have the question
of Scotland's future, because the Scots will

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:12.850
have voted to stay in the European Union,
and they may want to have another independence

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:16.250
referendum so they can stay in the EU but
be out of Britain.

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:17.250
There will be pressure for that.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.930
Whether it happens or not I don't know, but
there will be a question.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:23.680
Northern Ireland will be destabilized, because
its prosperity and its peace process is based

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:27.790
on a porous border with the south, and that
porous border would be in doubt.

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:32.650
There would be all kinds of regulations in
Britain that would have to be rewritten.

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:36.800
Some of these are to do with the trade relationship
with the most important trading partners,

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.370
which would be totally up in the air.

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:42.410
But there would also be domestic regulation
which derives from EU law that would have

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.330
to be redrafted.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:48.100
So you have this massive array of uncertainty.

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:54.250
And when you try to negotiate your way out
of that uncertainty into some new regime,

00:10:54.250 --> 00:11:01.450
you're depending on a talk with 27 other countries
which, as we've seen on refugees or the euro

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:04.180
crisis, are not super-fast about getting their
act together.

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:09.900
So the notion that you're going to resolve
this in less than two years seems pie in the

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:10.900
sky.

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:14.570
The president of the European Commission recently
said-European Council-recently said it could

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:16.220
take seven years.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.610
That might be too pessimistic.

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:23.050
But I think three to five years is a reasonable
projection.

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:27.020
The only precedent is Greenland leaving in
1985.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:31.590
Greenland had a grand total of, I think, 53,000
people at the time.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.600
It cared about one single issue, and that
was fish.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:40.140
And it was dealing with a much more simple
European Community when it left in '85.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:41.150
Britain is the second-biggest economy.

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.360
It's going to be a long negotiation.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:49.300
So I think the uncertainty is pervasive and
prolonged, and so the economic cost could

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.630
be higher than you can capture in forecasts.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:52.630
LEIGH: Thank you.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:58.470
Adam, how do you see the impact of the shock
that this would be, as Sebastian says, both

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:03.690
on the U.K. economy, its external balance
that's already rather fragile, and more generally

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:05.630
on the economy of the eurozone?

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:07.710
POSEN: Yes, thank you, Michael.

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.750
Thank you all for coming out.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.930
I very much agree with Sebastian, which will
probably not surprise you.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:18.910
But let me try to give a slightly different
perspective on why he's right and why it isn't

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:26.320
just a bunch of us CFR-types in conspiracy
with everyone who loves the queen trying to

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:30.610
preserve us from the Godless, the voters.

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:39.800
The first point to be recognized is this becomes
a huge downturn in the attractiveness of investment

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:42.430
in the U.K., and this comes from multiple
sources.

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:46.650
The first is the uncertainty that Sebastian
articulated, which is absolutely right.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.510
We're already seeing it in the data.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:52.090
We're already seeing a full stop to housebuilding
in the U.K.

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:55.600
We're already seeing a full stop to corporate
investment in the U.K.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:13:01.540
We're already seeing foreign investors of
large multinationals saying I can't go forward.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.170
And this uncertainty will only get worse.

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:09.220
We're also seeing uncertainty because this
is a regime shift.

00:13:09.220 --> 00:13:15.290
Now, the U.K. has prided itself for centuries
now on not really having a written constitution,

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.690
but this is effectively a change in constitution.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:22.640
This is-this is of that level of seriousness.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:25.910
And so the-and, as we know, for those of you
who have seen the musical "Hamilton" as well

00:13:25.910 --> 00:13:31.560
as other versions of American history, constitutional
shifts reflect the interests of who's in power

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.700
when you write the constitution.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:39.580
And if it is Boris Johnson, Michael Gove,
Nigel Farage, and their ilk who are in power,

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:47.440
writing the constitution, they are essentially
writing a nativist, as Sebastian rightly says,

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:52.540
fantasies of deregulation-and not all regulation's
bad, by the way-constitution that will be

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.380
very harmful to economics.

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:59.380
The third point is the specific EU relationship.

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:05.029
As Prime Minister Cameron said while I was
at the Bank of England, you know, the total

00:14:05.029 --> 00:14:12.430
amount of trade that the U.K. had with the
now-benighted BRICS was less than the total

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:15.560
amount of trade they have with Ireland.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:21.720
So this additional fantasy that somehow the
U.K. can completely reorient in the face of

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:26.040
the history and geography-that it doesn't
matter, somehow, that they're going to throw

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:32.420
up at a minimum uncertainty, and almost certainly
extra trade barriers with their huge market-for

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.970
centuries is absurd.

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:39.610
So this is why, sort of going back to something
Michael said, the campaign has become really

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:45.020
about immigration versus economics-sort of
status quo, are you shooting yourself in the

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:50.240
foot or the head, versus do you care about
people who don't look like you, making sure

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.080
they don't come to your town.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.690
And sadly, that is very much what this is
about.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.040
Two other points, if I could.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:03.300
On the economic shock in the short term, there
is a sense-and some people-and the Bank of

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.750
England has been very careful not to say what
they would do, understandably.

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:11.440
There is a sense among some people that, oh,
the Bank of England could ride to the rescue.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.570
And the currency may drop a lot, but that's
OK, it'll be stimulative, and the Bank of

00:15:16.570 --> 00:15:19.980
England-well, maybe they can't really cut
rates, but they can print more money and it'll

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.910
be OK.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:25.440
I think this is a very bad bet.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:32.880
I think-there are very few examples in history
we have of countries pulling themselves out

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:38.100
of peaceful trade relations and integration
with other countries.

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:43.820
Very few usually when there's been a revolution
or a right-wing coup or something.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:45.640
You have to go to, like, Latin America and
Mercosur and things like that.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:52.010
So the number of examples of this are very
few, but the record is you get a currency

00:15:52.010 --> 00:15:53.010
crisis.

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.880
You get a crash in the currency, and the central
bank basically has to put up interest rates

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:01.880
to keep inflation from going out of control,
and to keep from a downward spiral in confidence,

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:04.400
that no one's minding the store.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:11.430
And my bet is that the Bank of England might
initially try to hold off or possibly try

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:14.190
to do more stimulus, but in the end they would
have no choice.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:17.921
It would be-going back to Michael and the
point about a current account deficit, you're

00:16:17.921 --> 00:16:19.490
re-running some of the bad 1970s.

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:23.460
Now, obviously, you don't have a fixed exchange-rate
peg, so that buys you something.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:28.630
Obviously, the U.K. is a far more competitive
place now than it was 40 years ago.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:34.029
Nonetheless, the odds are instead you get
a capital flight and interest-rate hike in

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:35.029
a recession.

00:16:35.029 --> 00:16:37.860
The final point I raise is longer term.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.900
So, as Sebastian indicated, there is a longer-term
growth effect here.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:46.779
And if this investment just fails for a few
years, that already compounds the recession

00:16:46.779 --> 00:16:50.490
and the cost of the foregone investment we
already had due to the crisis.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.040
That's bad enough.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:58.120
But if you are fundamentally changing the
U.K., the question becomes, what is your economic

00:16:58.120 --> 00:16:59.300
model going forward?

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:07.189
My concern is in part based on comments by
the leadership of the pro-Brexit vote, is

00:17:07.189 --> 00:17:11.459
that they essentially double down on being
a financial center; that they say we want

00:17:11.459 --> 00:17:15.579
to be the offshore financial center for Europe;
oh, look, the Chinese are going to start trading

00:17:15.579 --> 00:17:19.329
renminbi denominated in London, it's going
to be great.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:23.740
Well, there are three major problems with
this.

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:29.020
The first is that's actually not done very
well for the U.K. in recent years.

00:17:29.020 --> 00:17:33.289
As Mervyn King used to say, this has completely
unbalanced the British economy.

00:17:33.289 --> 00:17:40.149
That's why you have such wealth and such concentration
in London, and such relative impoverishization

00:17:40.149 --> 00:17:42.850
of other industries in other regions of the
U.K.

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:44.110
It's just a huge distortion.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:45.789
It tends to overvalue the currency.

00:17:45.789 --> 00:17:49.330
It tends to move things in the wrong way on
the current account.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:51.649
It's very bad.

00:17:51.649 --> 00:17:55.980
Second, despite what Governor Carney said
a couple years ago about being willing to

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:02.629
have bank balance sheets at 900 percent of
GDP, the fact is, as Iceland and Ireland Portugal

00:18:02.629 --> 00:18:08.150
have shown, it's not a good idea to have banks
that are many, many times larger than your

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:13.369
GDP because you're left holding the bag as
taxpayers when something goes wrong.

00:18:13.369 --> 00:18:18.090
And so, if the U.K. goes down this avenue,
they are just exposing themselves more and

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:20.059
more to future risks.

00:18:20.059 --> 00:18:23.630
The third reason why I'm concerned about this
is because I think they'll be desperate.

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:29.059
If there is this kind of recession we're talking
about, even in the short term, if they do-after

00:18:29.059 --> 00:18:33.919
promising, oh, this is going to help the queue
times at the NHS, but actually they end up

00:18:33.919 --> 00:18:38.779
having to cut further the NHS, they're going
to be desperate to do something.

00:18:38.779 --> 00:18:42.940
And the banks-the investment banks, the major
commercial banks-are already talking about

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:47.450
pulling out of London, and they're going to
lose some business to probably Dublin, not

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:52.009
Frankfurt, because the euro area trading and
stuff in the eurozone will want to take place

00:18:52.009 --> 00:18:54.769
inside Europe.

00:18:54.769 --> 00:18:59.419
So then, if you're anti-regulator fantasists
to begin with, you start going down the path

00:18:59.419 --> 00:19:01.909
of, oh, we can become an even more offshore
center.

00:19:01.909 --> 00:19:06.039
We can become the Cayman Islands writ large,
or Panama writ large.

00:19:06.039 --> 00:19:10.150
And this, frankly, is a way I think it also
spills over to the rest of the world, is that

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:13.379
the U.K. decides, hey, regulatory arbitrage.

00:19:13.379 --> 00:19:18.809
Letting AIG financial products run in London
actually destroyed the U.S. financial system;

00:19:18.809 --> 00:19:21.009
didn't hurt us, made us a lot of money.

00:19:21.009 --> 00:19:22.419
Let's continue down this path.

00:19:22.419 --> 00:19:27.169
Let us be the race-to-the-bottom financial
center.

00:19:27.169 --> 00:19:33.120
And I think that that's where this is going
because they're not going to have any other

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:34.120
option.

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:36.460
LEIGH: And, Constanze, cheer us all up.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:37.580
STELZENM�LLER: You want me for the cheerful
note?

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.250
I knew where this was going.

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:40.250
(Laughter.)

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:41.250
LEIGH: Cheer us all up.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:42.250
I mean, the EU-
POSEN: Look how cheerful!

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:43.250
(Laughter.)

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:44.549
LEIGH: Nice, smile early in the morning.

00:19:44.549 --> 00:19:45.580
It's always helpful.

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:51.720
But the EU is facing any number of crises
or disorders, as I call them, as they last

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:56.140
for longer than a brief, acute crisis.

00:19:56.140 --> 00:20:01.830
Whether the lingering uncertainties related
to the eurozone, eurozone governance, banking

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:06.509
union, fiscal union, all in abeyance for the
time being.

00:20:06.509 --> 00:20:12.389
The migration issue, which has become top
of the agenda in Brussels.

00:20:12.389 --> 00:20:19.770
Lingering problems over sanctions, Ukraine,
Russia, Putin's efforts to divide Europe in

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:24.750
various ways, and indeed to support some of
the populists in Europe who are opposed to

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:27.409
the European Union in different ways.

00:20:27.409 --> 00:20:32.820
Instability around the Mediterranean, North
Africa, state failure in Libya.

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.320
New waves of migration, possibly-
STELZENM�LLER: Now, where's the cheerful

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:36.320
part?

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:37.320
(Laughter.)

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:38.320
LEIGH: Very, very cheerful picture.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:46.700
And, I mean, how would Brexit affect the EU's
capacity to deal with these other challenges?

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:52.610
And also, what would it mean to the internal
balance of power, so to speak, within the

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.040
EU?

00:20:54.040 --> 00:21:01.259
And would a certain country that you know
well become more preponderant than it is today

00:21:01.259 --> 00:21:03.740
if Britain were to withdraw?

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.039
In two-and-a-half minutes, please.

00:21:05.039 --> 00:21:06.039
(Laughter.)

00:21:06.039 --> 00:21:07.039
STELZENM�LLER: Thank you.

00:21:07.039 --> 00:21:08.039
POSEN: I yield my time.

00:21:08.039 --> 00:21:10.749
STELZENM�LLER: So what Michael has done-has
just done in a number of ways is raise the

00:21:10.749 --> 00:21:14.120
German question, just to make this very clear,
since I am German.

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:23.710
However, let me start with a personal note,
if I may, just like you.

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:26.649
I am German, completely, but have the benefit
of an English childhood, and of some childhood

00:21:26.649 --> 00:21:27.690
years in America.

00:21:27.690 --> 00:21:33.500
So I feel like an insider/outsider in this
whole story.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:43.530
And I am old enough a German to remember vividly
that while the loadstars of the early decades

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:49.429
of the German postwar renewal were, of course,
reconciliation with France and Poland, later

00:21:49.429 --> 00:21:56.299
on, and those were very, very important and
still are, the countries that we wanted to

00:21:56.299 --> 00:21:59.909
be like were Great Britain and America.

00:21:59.909 --> 00:22:11.100
Those were-that was what we wanted to become-liberal,
outward looking, relaxed, normal was the word

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:16.549
used then, countries that engage in Europe,
that engage in the world.

00:22:16.549 --> 00:22:23.899
And that in general, you know, are seen as
a force for good, both in their neighborhood,

00:22:23.899 --> 00:22:28.990
and on the continent, and in the world.

00:22:28.990 --> 00:22:36.269
And I think for a-for generations of post-war
German politicians this was going to London,

00:22:36.269 --> 00:22:43.639
going to Washington, developing networks there,
modeling themselves on the people that they

00:22:43.639 --> 00:22:45.019
met there.

00:22:45.019 --> 00:22:53.279
And that-and a very much smaller scale, included
me-was the way to move our country towards

00:22:53.279 --> 00:22:54.279
a better place.

00:22:54.279 --> 00:23:00.919
And of course, as you said, Germany these
days suddenly finds itself in a position of

00:23:00.919 --> 00:23:04.679
what The Economist has called the reluctant
hegemon in Europe.

00:23:04.679 --> 00:23:07.690
The hegemony part is only moderately persuasive,
I think.

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:10.690
The reluctance, yes, still there.

00:23:10.690 --> 00:23:16.100
That said, some of you will have noticed that
Berlin is trying to be a bit more forward

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:19.940
leaning of late, particularly since the three
speeches in Munich.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:24.880
And I think seen from Berlin right now, what
this looks like a really ghastly mid-life

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:30.350
crisis divorce, where essentially your husband
who you deeply love, and you've been with

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:33.909
together, and you've got children, and you're
got a house, and you've got a car, and you've

00:23:33.909 --> 00:23:37.759
got history and stories is saying, you know
what?

00:23:37.759 --> 00:23:38.759
I want to buy the Porsche.

00:23:38.759 --> 00:23:40.739
And I want to rip around with it.

00:23:40.739 --> 00:23:42.039
And you know what?

00:23:42.039 --> 00:23:45.860
If I drive it into the wall, that's OK too,
because I need to do this now.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:46.860
And you're saying, all right.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:51.629
You know, if you-darling, if you really need
to do this, I think at some point you're just

00:23:51.629 --> 00:23:54.500
going to have to go out and trash the car.

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:56.559
But meanwhile, I've got stuff to do.

00:23:56.559 --> 00:24:01.299
You know, I think that's the-(laughter)-you
know, the children still need to finish school

00:24:01.299 --> 00:24:04.400
and university, you know?

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:07.539
And the garden still needs to be taken care
of.

00:24:07.539 --> 00:24:09.149
And there are larger issues here.

00:24:09.149 --> 00:24:14.889
So I mean, I'm caricaturing slightly for the
purposes of making this clear.

00:24:14.889 --> 00:24:23.109
I think in Berlin there's a sense that a great
many concessions were made to London over

00:24:23.109 --> 00:24:26.330
time, in terms of loosening London's commitment
to Europe.

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:29.919
And it's got exemptions on many, many accounts.

00:24:29.919 --> 00:24:36.869
And I think on that-on that point certainly,
I think the relationship has reached the end

00:24:36.869 --> 00:24:37.869
of its tether.

00:24:37.869 --> 00:24:42.289
People who say now, oh, you know, Britain
never got the concessions that it wanted,

00:24:42.289 --> 00:24:44.039
that is simply not true.

00:24:44.039 --> 00:24:47.299
But would Britain's absence in Europe change
things?

00:24:47.299 --> 00:24:48.299
Of course it would.

00:24:48.299 --> 00:24:54.070
I mean, not just because, as we all know,
Britain had a very, very important role both

00:24:54.070 --> 00:25:00.049
internally and in the external relations of
Europe as the anchor of the transatlantic

00:25:00.049 --> 00:25:04.929
relationship, as the guarantor and indeed
the leader, together with France, in a more

00:25:04.929 --> 00:25:07.669
forward-leaning foreign security policy.

00:25:07.669 --> 00:25:13.539
And yes, of course, as a balancer between
France and Germany and the Franco-German axis,

00:25:13.539 --> 00:25:19.320
which itself has become somewhat lopsided
of late because of France's going through

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:23.399
a more inward-looking moment of its own.

00:25:23.399 --> 00:25:29.600
And so there is a-look, I'm not going to-I'm
not going to gild the lily here.

00:25:29.600 --> 00:25:32.609
I'm think people are terrified.

00:25:32.609 --> 00:25:33.809
I'm terrified.

00:25:33.809 --> 00:25:40.320
I think that this could-there's two ways this
could go.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:45.840
And these have been speculated upon, but as
Sebastian said, it's very hard to calculate

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:47.830
uncertainty, not just on the economic front.

00:25:47.830 --> 00:25:50.950
In some ways it's even more difficult in foreign
security policy.

00:25:50.950 --> 00:25:52.059
There's two models.

00:25:52.059 --> 00:25:56.419
One, we all rush towards deeper integration
in one way or another.

00:25:56.419 --> 00:26:00.830
And certainly there are fields in which you
would think that this would be an obvious

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:05.220
thing to do with or without a Brexit.

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:11.909
The euro zone being the most obvious case,
and where you could say we are in fact at

00:26:11.909 --> 00:26:12.909
a halfway house which needs to be completed.

00:26:12.909 --> 00:26:16.749
But you could also say security and foreign
policy and defense, indeed.

00:26:16.749 --> 00:26:19.179
You could say migration policy.

00:26:19.179 --> 00:26:25.700
And you could indeed, I think of late, you
would add justice, and home affairs, and intelligence,

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:28.570
and counterterrorism cooperation to that list.

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:30.700
That's one option.

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:37.700
Sch�uble, my country's finance minister,
has recently said that he was absolutely opposed

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:40.350
to this, which I have to say came as a surprise
to me.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.769
I had always expected his house to be rather
for that.

00:26:43.769 --> 00:26:46.379
Maybe it's being technical here.

00:26:46.379 --> 00:26:53.669
I did hear a senior Southern European diplomat
at a lunch here in Washington say recently

00:26:53.669 --> 00:26:58.989
that if Brexit happened, they would come to
the June 28th council meeting with a radical

00:26:58.989 --> 00:27:05.809
proposal for deeper integration, which I have
to say I was surprised by because I'm not

00:27:05.809 --> 00:27:07.670
sure there are many takers for this currently
in Paris and Berlin.

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:14.460
And so the other alternative is either, you
know, chaos and muddling through or disintegration

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:19.659
as populist movement and politicians in other
European countries take a cue from Brexit

00:27:19.659 --> 00:27:22.799
and try to take off on their own.

00:27:22.799 --> 00:27:25.960
I think that would be disastrous for Europe,
and I think it would be disastrous for the

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:26.960
United States.

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:31.950
That said, if it-if the United States is run
by a President Trump, I'm not sure Washington

00:27:31.950 --> 00:27:32.950
will care.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:33.950
LEIGH: (Laughs.)

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:34.950
Thanks, Constanze.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:39.090
Picking up on the last comment you made, about
the impact on other countries, I wanted to

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:46.669
ask Sebastian about the risk of contagion
to other EU countries if Britain were to leave.

00:27:46.669 --> 00:27:55.570
Would we then see similar demands arising
in Denmark, in Poland, in France, in Italy?

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.950
There have been signs of this.

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:00.009
Do you see a domino effect?

00:28:00.009 --> 00:28:04.549
MALLABY: I think that they said there's two
kinds of contagion that might take place.

00:28:04.549 --> 00:28:11.059
The one that's most commonly cited is that
there are populist movements, which are often

00:28:11.059 --> 00:28:14.859
anti-EU, across the European Union.

00:28:14.859 --> 00:28:20.769
And if you look at euro barometer polls, it's
very striking that the countries where people

00:28:20.769 --> 00:28:26.149
appear to be most anti-EU, first is Greece,
for obvious reasons have to do with austerity,

00:28:26.149 --> 00:28:28.369
and second is France, not Britain.

00:28:28.369 --> 00:28:29.480
Britain is third.

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:36.309
So the French voters appear to be more skeptical
of the EU than the Brits.

00:28:36.309 --> 00:28:41.970
And of course, there are apparently plenty
of people in France who are willing to vote

00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:48.840
for Marine Le Pen, who will probably be in
the runoff in the presidential race next year.

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:55.350
So the French establishment sees all this
and is obliged to take a pretty stiff line

00:28:55.350 --> 00:29:01.309
to, I think, to-this plays into the chaos
and uncertainty point I was highlighting before.

00:29:01.309 --> 00:29:07.320
If Britain thinks it can easily negotiate
a deal with the rest of Europe on the terms

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:14.100
of its departure, it's got another thing coming,
because the French need-they don't just want-they

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:20.820
need to punish the U.K. to send a message
to their own domestic populists who would

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:22.269
like to follow Britain's example.

00:29:22.269 --> 00:29:26.010
So that's one type of contagion, is to the
populace elsewhere.

00:29:26.010 --> 00:29:34.509
But the other sort, it strikes me, is that
other EU members that are no in the euro zone-Denmark,

00:29:34.509 --> 00:29:41.229
most obviously-are going to feel uncomfortable
because they worry, understandably, that the

00:29:41.229 --> 00:29:48.450
integration that Constanze talks about, which
is the logical-you know, we have a halfway

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.549
house in terms of the euro.

00:29:50.549 --> 00:29:51.970
And we need to complete the building.

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:55.590
And that means further integration of fiscal
policy and so forth.

00:29:55.590 --> 00:30:00.570
If you're not in the euro zone, that deeper
integration is a threat.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:04.509
You feel like the insider club is going to
make decisions that they impose on you.

00:30:04.509 --> 00:30:09.139
At least if its Britain and Denmark and a
few others, there's a bloc can that push back

00:30:09.139 --> 00:30:10.200
against that.

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:16.679
I think if Britain leaves and the most insistent
and weighty advocate of the don't squash us

00:30:16.679 --> 00:30:21.320
non-euro brigade is gone, the rest of the
non-euros become rather vulnerable.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:27.690
POSEN: Can I piggyback on some more narrow
economic transmission mechanisms?

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:39.639
Because I think-I think Constanze's dissolving
marriage analogy is very apt, as well as very

00:30:39.639 --> 00:30:40.639
lively.

00:30:40.639 --> 00:30:44.710
Integration is a world we toss around a lot,
but the degree of integration of the U.K.

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:47.159
with the EU economy is very large.

00:30:47.159 --> 00:30:50.190
And so it is a lose-lose.

00:30:50.190 --> 00:30:57.700
I completely agree with Sebastian and Constanze
that both the game theory logic and the actual

00:30:57.700 --> 00:31:04.119
statements in private and public of senior
European officials is we cannot let this be

00:31:04.119 --> 00:31:06.019
easy for the U.K.

00:31:06.019 --> 00:31:08.029
There's not going to be a nice divorce here.

00:31:08.029 --> 00:31:14.659
We need a settlement that is-that pays me
alimony for all that I gave up all those years.

00:31:14.659 --> 00:31:15.659
(Laughter.)

00:31:15.659 --> 00:31:21.919
And putting it very crudely, I mean, it's-if
you have 27 nations, think of it in congressional

00:31:21.919 --> 00:31:22.919
terms.

00:31:22.919 --> 00:31:25.820
Any deal is going to have to basically be
approved by all 27.

00:31:25.820 --> 00:31:32.479
So each one is going to just simply take its
own little veto point and say-if I'm Denmark,

00:31:32.479 --> 00:31:34.099
I want something on agriculture.

00:31:34.099 --> 00:31:36.789
If I'm Ireland, I want something on border
control.

00:31:36.789 --> 00:31:41.230
If I'm Italy, I want something-god knows what-I
want money.

00:31:41.230 --> 00:31:42.230
(Laughter.)

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:46.299
But whatever it is, they're each going to
try to extract something, as well as having

00:31:46.299 --> 00:31:48.899
this desire to deter others.

00:31:48.899 --> 00:31:55.249
And that is logical, but it's also harmful
to Europe-it's harmful to Europe itself.

00:31:55.249 --> 00:31:58.620
It may not feel it has a choice, and this
may be where it comes out.

00:31:58.620 --> 00:32:01.880
But it's harmful to European trade and European
economies.

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:09.230
It raises the level of uncertainty about investment
and integration-excuse me-within Europe.

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:11.979
And then you start having to think through
all the things that have to be untangled.

00:32:11.979 --> 00:32:13.240
So let me give you a couple examples.

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:15.279
So we've mentioned Ireland.

00:32:15.279 --> 00:32:18.950
As Sebastian pointed out, as my colleague
Jacob Kirkegaard at the Peterson Instituted

00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:23.809
was among the first to point out, you're going
to institute this border between Northern

00:32:23.809 --> 00:32:29.029
Ireland and Ireland, that reopens entirely
the peace process, the questions, all that.

00:32:29.029 --> 00:32:34.980
But you also are doing huge damage to the
Northern Ireland economy and some major disruption

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:36.700
to the Irish economy.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:41.159
At the same time, you probably have a nontrivial
number-because remember, Dublin's kind of

00:32:41.159 --> 00:32:46.149
small-a nontrivial number of financial services
and other industries who want to move their

00:32:46.149 --> 00:32:49.919
headquarters from London to Dublin because
it's English speaking, rule of law, short

00:32:49.919 --> 00:32:54.320
flight, they can even still live in London
if they want, but do that.

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:57.389
So Ireland gets completely whipsawed in different
ways.

00:32:57.389 --> 00:32:58.389
Spain.

00:32:58.389 --> 00:33:04.059
There are a million U.K., mostly retirees,
living in Spain.

00:33:04.059 --> 00:33:09.190
They are not going to have access to the health
care in Spain paid for anymore.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:10.610
What happens to them?

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:13.389
Do they all move back to the U.K.?

00:33:13.389 --> 00:33:18.399
Which is an interesting form of not immigration,
but which is going to do more to bankrupt

00:33:18.399 --> 00:33:23.029
the NHS than any sort of Polish plumber of
working age.

00:33:23.029 --> 00:33:29.039
Do they dump their real estate on the market
in Spain because they can't live there and

00:33:29.039 --> 00:33:30.580
count on health care anymore?

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:34.399
Or what happens to the Spanish real estate
market, which has just recovered from all

00:33:34.399 --> 00:33:36.260
the turmoil?

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:37.260
Poland.

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:41.049
Now, this gets more into the kinds of things
Sebastian was talking about.

00:33:41.049 --> 00:33:44.529
You have a right-wing government that has
been increasingly going right-wing.

00:33:44.529 --> 00:33:52.169
They took off on this overhyped anti-neoliberalism
thing from the IMF and made a public statement,

00:33:52.169 --> 00:33:56.120
well, we're basically recanting everything
we've done for the last 30 years, even though

00:33:56.120 --> 00:34:00.570
that made us the best-performing economy in
Eastern Europe.

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:06.460
You start getting this momentum between Poland
and Orban in Hungary of not just populism-which

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:16.530
frankly is the more serious part, I agree-but
of anti-liberal economic policies.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:22.669
And then, of course, in the financial markets
you start pricing back in breakup risk in

00:34:22.669 --> 00:34:24.940
the euro area.

00:34:24.940 --> 00:34:29.130
So it does come down to what Constanze mentioned.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:33.680
Can the EU turn this into some whole new integration
project?

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:39.590
And that has been the theory of functionalism
that we've always had with the EU.

00:34:39.590 --> 00:34:40.960
There's a crisis.

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:45.570
We respond by doing more.

00:34:45.570 --> 00:34:50.169
I am very scared that this time, at least,
it will be a long lag before that happens.

00:34:50.169 --> 00:34:52.190
And there'll be a lot broken as a result.

00:34:52.190 --> 00:34:53.600
LEIGH: I think you're right.

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:57.530
Well, the time has come to open up to your
questions and comments or remarks.

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:00.050
We have about 20 minutes.

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:01.760
Please introduce yourself very briefly.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:06.290
And if you wish to address your question to
one of the panel, please indicate.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:07.320
Yes, sir.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:13.640
Q: Yes, Mike Elliott from the ONE Campaign,
and before that many other affiliations, the

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:15.940
Economist, as Sebastian.

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:22.500
Some time ago I wrote and presented the definitive
BBC series on Britain and the U.K. called

00:35:22.500 --> 00:35:26.020
The Poison Chalice, which has just been reshown,
I'm pleased to say, on BBC TV.

00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:31.060
And of course, I agree with everything that
Constanze and Adam and Sebastian have said.

00:35:31.060 --> 00:35:35.530
But as I used to be a kind of full-time journalist
rather than part-time one, let me play devil's

00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:37.580
advocate for a second.

00:35:37.580 --> 00:35:43.820
Would the panelists concede that, as President
Tusk and Finance Minister Sch�uble have

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:52.530
both in the last week or so sort of hinted,
that for 20 years the response in Brussels

00:35:52.530 --> 00:36:01.130
to every crisis that's come along of more
Europe has, if you like, exacerbated an existing

00:36:01.130 --> 00:36:09.850
suspicion in the U.K. and elsewhere that nothing
that people say to stop more Europe is every

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:10.850
listened to?

00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:17.620
We've had referendums in-I'll lose count-France,
Denmark, Ireland-which have essentially been

00:36:17.620 --> 00:36:22.020
kind of treated by the European establishment
as an excuse to have another referendum until

00:36:22.020 --> 00:36:23.950
you get the right question.

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:32.990
So is there any-is there any fault to be laid
at the feet of the European institutions for

00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:36.610
not taking what are now called populist pressures
seriously?

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:37.610
MALLABY: Yes.

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:38.850
LEIGH: Sebastian, go ahead.

00:36:38.850 --> 00:36:43.710
MALLABY: Well, look, Michael, this is a very
good question, as I would expect from you.

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:45.110
And I think the answer is yes.

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:50.450
I mean, in some ways Europe seems to me to
be an experiment in different types of cross-border

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:51.600
integration.

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:57.130
We know that in a world with lots of trans-border
threats, you need trans-border institutions.

00:36:57.130 --> 00:37:01.430
And we probably have, in aggregate in the
world, too few not too many.

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:05.731
So in general, you know, the Bretton Woods
institutions, I think one could have more

00:37:05.731 --> 00:37:06.731
of them not less.

00:37:06.731 --> 00:37:10.800
But in the case of Europe, it's a case where
sometimes the integration has been too ambitious

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:12.990
and has overshot.

00:37:12.990 --> 00:37:15.000
I think that's clearly the case with a single
currency.

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:19.250
And you know, a lot of the trouble that we
see how and a lot of the arguments being wheeled

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:24.820
out in Britain about why we should leave the
club are precisely that this is a club of

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:25.840
economic failure.

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:26.980
Why do we want to be a member of that?

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:31.260
And the economic failure is not because of
the single market, which has been fantastic.

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.880
The single market is an example of great integration.

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:35.500
But the single currency is not.

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:36.780
It's the opposite.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:42.490
So I think that the problem I suspect if you
brought the Euro elites into, you know, a

00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:50.230
padded room with no one listening to them
and you said, how, was the euro a mistake?

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:51.810
You know, they would probably say yes.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:54.560
But you can't go back very easily, as we've
seen with the Greek dilemma.

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:56.470
So one is stuck.

00:37:56.470 --> 00:37:58.380
The house is half-built.

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:03.770
Do you fire the contractor when they've knocked
all your walls down and say I don't like you

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:04.770
anymore?

00:38:04.770 --> 00:38:05.770
It's quite difficult to do.

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:11.140
So you-there is this sort of logic of moving
forward, which both Adam and Constanze have

00:38:11.140 --> 00:38:12.140
referred to.

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:17.730
But it's not clear, at least in the single
currency case, that that's ultimately going

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:19.420
to work.

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:25.580
STELZENM�LLER: Well, you know, Michael,
as I was saying earlier, I think the EU and

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:31.900
leading nations in Europe have already made
an awful lot of concessions to Britain.

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:36.880
And I think in retrospect, it seems to me
that successive British governments have done

00:38:36.880 --> 00:38:38.920
a fair lot of moving of goal posts.

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:44.740
And I think it's-I think, you know, one is
entitled to say that.

00:38:44.740 --> 00:38:56.330
I also think that the leavers, it seems to
me, recklessly disregard the positive side

00:38:56.330 --> 00:39:00.390
of European integration and the impact that
that has had on them.

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:07.700
The degree of peace, prosperity, and democratic
transformation that was brought to Europe,

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:09.210
and that it of course has a benefit to them.

00:39:09.210 --> 00:39:13.430
I mean, it's-one of the mindboggling things
to me, having not just grown up with British-you

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:18.820
know, in sort of British surroundings, but
sort of somewhat consciously sought out as

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:24.220
I grew older and sort of as a journalist,
you know, connections and networks to think-you

00:39:24.220 --> 00:39:26.290
know, how does one do these things?

00:39:26.290 --> 00:39:31.480
Looking at British civil servants and journalists
and thinking this is what we ought to be doing.

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:37.870
How the imagination-the political imagination
and breadth of vision of successive generations

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:44.610
of British politicians and policymakers seem
to have shrunk within my lifetime.

00:39:44.610 --> 00:39:51.330
And I can't quite explain to you how that
has happened, but frankly I don't think Europe

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:56.500
should take the blame because whenever I travel
around Europe, and I do a great deal of that,

00:39:56.500 --> 00:40:01.940
particularly to the periphery, I see transformations
which are miraculous and which are worth fighting

00:40:01.940 --> 00:40:06.900
for, and which I believe my generation of
Germans is now trying very hard to fight for,

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:09.630
but which we're not going to be able to do
on our own.

00:40:09.630 --> 00:40:12.360
And I mean, case in point, Turkey.

00:40:12.360 --> 00:40:19.590
The leave campaign is now waging a vicious
and completely disingenuous campaign based

00:40:19.590 --> 00:40:24.130
on the supposition that millions of Turks
are standing at Calais just waiting to swim

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:25.580
over, you know?

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:27.680
It was the Brits who worked hardest.

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:36.320
Who in fact-you know, were wielding a crowbar
in order to get Turkey on a membership path,

00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:40.300
over the strong objections of many other Europeans.

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:41.770
That is now completely forgotten.

00:40:41.770 --> 00:40:45.570
And you know, I could go on, but that just
gives you one example.

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:47.710
So I'm going to disagree slightly with Sebastian.

00:40:47.710 --> 00:40:52.170
LEIGH: Perhaps we'll give others an opportunity
to pose their questions.

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:53.470
Yes, please.

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:55.420
Q: Thank you.

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:58.670
Doug Rediker, International Capital Strategies.

00:40:58.670 --> 00:41:03.160
So the discussion so far has been all premised
on the idea that the Brexit vote takes place

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:04.770
for the leave campaign to win.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:09.370
I'd just like to ask the panelists, what happens
if the remain campaign wins?

00:41:09.370 --> 00:41:10.410
Is it business as usual the next morning?

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:13.820
What is the lasting damage?

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:20.810
Or what is the lasting consequence of a successful
remain campaign, both within the U.K. political

00:41:20.810 --> 00:41:23.740
system, and then more broadly across Europe?

00:41:23.740 --> 00:41:24.740
LEIGH: Sebastian.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:29.460
MALLABY: So I think one thing that's happened
during this campaign is that Prime Minister

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:32.680
Cameron began by saying he didn't want to
have blue-on-blue attacks.

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:37.310
He would be restrained in what he said about
Boris Johnson and Michael Grove.

00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:40.380
He now calls them liars pretty much straight
out.

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:46.950
So the degree of vitriolic poison within the
governing party, at a time when the opposition

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:51.710
is run by a far-left guy with no governing
potential, means that there is a political

00:41:51.710 --> 00:41:55.770
vacuum in Britain which would be damaging,
even if we vote to remain.

00:41:55.770 --> 00:42:00.270
It's also the case that Cameron has promised
that he will step down by 2020, which in effect

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:06.950
means he has to go in 2019 so that his successor
has a chance before the 2020 election to get

00:42:06.950 --> 00:42:09.020
into the office and get ready.

00:42:09.020 --> 00:42:14.890
So there'll be a three-year run between this
referendum and a leadership transition.

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:17.650
And so therefore, we're already in it.

00:42:17.650 --> 00:42:21.440
And it's very likely that the majority of
the Conservative Party rank and file will

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:27.470
have voted clearly for leaving the EU, even
if the country has voted to remain.

00:42:27.470 --> 00:42:31.730
So therefore, the next leader in the Conservative
Party, reflecting the views of his party,

00:42:31.730 --> 00:42:37.800
is likely to be a leaver, probably Boris Johnson,
who has demonstrated a capacity for making

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:40.520
stuff up to an amazing degree.

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:43.260
And so I don't think this issue goes away.

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:48.340
In the course of becoming leader of the Conservative
Party, the next person will probably say things

00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:53.460
in the campaign which, although not quite
promising a second referendum, will kind of

00:42:53.460 --> 00:42:56.930
harrumph that, you know, of course we know
we should have left.

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:01.310
So I think this British question, a bit like
the Scottish question after the referendum

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:07.870
in 2014 where the vote was to remain the U.K.,
but immediately the Scottish nationalist party

00:43:07.870 --> 00:43:12.470
did even better in the Scottish elections
and in terms of Westminster representation.

00:43:12.470 --> 00:43:13.620
I think the same thing happens here.

00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:18.560
The Conservative Party continues on its anti-EU
trajectory.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:23.470
Even if we remain, that's bad for the sense
of stability in the relationship.

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:30.250
I signed a petition actually just yesterday
precisely about this point, which is, you

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:34.321
know, if we do vote to remain, for goodness
sake, let's not go back to the old British

00:43:34.321 --> 00:43:39.030
way of carping and, you know, pointing the
finger and scoring cheap points at the expense

00:43:39.030 --> 00:43:40.030
of Brussels.

00:43:40.030 --> 00:43:41.960
Let's not leave, but let's lead.

00:43:41.960 --> 00:43:46.850
Let's get engaged and try to build, you know,
a constructive agenda of EU cooperation on

00:43:46.850 --> 00:43:48.800
stuff that matters for our kids.

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:57.230
But I'm afraid-you know, it's a good petition,
but I see the political-

00:43:57.230 --> 00:43:59.070
POSEN: Can I-sorry.

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:03.510
Let me be more boring and more optimistic,
with one exception.

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:08.310
The historical record, including the Scotland
vote, the Parti Qu�b�cois vote, house

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:16.170
votes on the Front National in France, in
general is you do go back to business as usual.

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:22.680
I think you get a brief run-up in sterling
and in stocks in the U.K., and whatever.

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:25.700
Nothing material of long-lasting significance.

00:44:25.700 --> 00:44:32.590
I think the status quo bias is at least as
strong for businesses as it is for voters.

00:44:32.590 --> 00:44:34.540
There will be a sigh of relief.

00:44:34.540 --> 00:44:38.950
I don't gainsay anything Sebastian says about
internal British politics.

00:44:38.950 --> 00:44:45.580
Where I want to pick up on Constanze, and
then she can correct me, is I think that the

00:44:45.580 --> 00:44:50.990
real fraying is U.K.-intra-U.K.-EU that maybe
there's not this trust.

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:54.400
Maybe there's a sigh of relief.

00:44:54.400 --> 00:45:00.310
Maybe it's an agreement to live separately
but not fight in front of the kids.

00:45:00.310 --> 00:45:01.310
(Laughter.)

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:04.470
But essentially, there ain't going to be that
many more special deals for the U.K.

00:45:04.470 --> 00:45:11.500
I mean, there may be a little bit of-if the
U.K. aligns itself with Denmark and Poland

00:45:11.500 --> 00:45:16.020
and creates a euro out bloc, maybe something,
but there's just not going to be much there.

00:45:16.020 --> 00:45:24.200
The final thing I would say is I think the
biggest positive upside is I've been very

00:45:24.200 --> 00:45:27.050
worried, obviously, about the Trump scenario.

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:31.600
Both Donald and I will be in London the day
after the vote.

00:45:31.600 --> 00:45:36.460
I assume he will get more press coverage than
I do.

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:37.460
(Laughter.)

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:39.120
He has a better tan.

00:45:39.120 --> 00:45:47.390
But you know, I am very afraid that a Brexit
vote essentially legitimatizes nativist, populist

00:45:47.390 --> 00:45:48.450
movements.

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:54.710
So if you go back to history and you look
at Mussolini roughly 1929, there were a lot

00:45:54.710 --> 00:45:59.410
of people-including in the U.K., including
in Germany, including in the U.S. who said

00:45:59.410 --> 00:46:01.940
the classic, you know, he makes the trains
run on time.

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:08.970
It was legitimating for Hitler and the Nazis
in the '30s that, oh, yeah, these people say

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:13.930
that out of office and they're terrible, but
in the end they come and nothing horrible

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:15.730
happens.

00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:22.660
And so to me, the big effect-a big effect,
I shouldn't say the-a big effect of a remain

00:46:22.660 --> 00:46:28.830
vote is we can continue to say these are people
outside the spectrum.

00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:30.960
And that applies to Trump.

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:36.010
STELZENM�LLER: I'd like to jump in here,
because I think I'm going to be more pessimistic.

00:46:36.010 --> 00:46:38.370
A, you know how it is with a near divorce?

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:44.170
You've just taken a long, hard look at your
husband and said, shit, you know, this isn't

00:46:44.170 --> 00:46:46.670
the guy I married.

00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:51.610
And you know, you may still live in the same
house, but it's a good bet that you're going

00:46:51.610 --> 00:46:53.590
to have separate beds.

00:46:53.590 --> 00:47:01.250
And I think the most disturbing thing about
the referendum campaign in Britain and, indeed,

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:08.970
the American election campaign, has been the
figures, the ideas, and political positions

00:47:08.970 --> 00:47:11.730
that have suddenly been aired in public.

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:17.120
And there, I would say there is a real difference
between populism in Great Britain-you had

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:20.520
asked us this before-and in America and in
my own country.

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:26.200
We, of course, have the alternative for Germany,
which is a nasty lot of people who are flirting

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:29.010
with the extreme rights in overt ways.

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:32.400
But our parties are sort of congregating in
the middle.

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:34.500
They're all jammed in the middle.

00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:40.210
And extreme positions don't really have much
of a-of attraction.

00:47:40.210 --> 00:47:47.890
One of the great unhappinesses of the left
party in Germany has been that it really has

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:51.120
gotten very little traction about all that's
going on.

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:55.820
And in fact, left party politicians have been
looking at Bernie Sanders wistfully saying:

00:47:55.820 --> 00:48:01.090
You know, we never thought it could happen
in America.

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:07.230
And whereas here, you have Jeremy Corbyn,
as you said, who has sidekicks like Seumas

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:15.400
Milne, you know, who are the epitome of gold-spoon,
you know, British upper-middle class educations

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:16.400
and upbringings.

00:48:16.400 --> 00:48:23.250
And who, from what I can see of his previous
writings-this is the public affairs man of

00:48:23.250 --> 00:48:26.200
Corbyn who used to be a leader writer for
The Guardian.

00:48:26.200 --> 00:48:30.680
If you read his stuff, it's pretty much mainline
Trotskyism, which I find astonishing.

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:34.000
I mean, you couldn't do this kind of thing
in Germany.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:35.000
People would laugh in his face.

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:42.030
And what I find astonishing is that people
like Sanders, and Corbyn, and Milne, and Trump,

00:48:42.030 --> 00:48:44.700
can even get so far in countries which used
to be sane.

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:48.350
I mean, that's what I'd like to see somebody
explain to me.

00:48:48.350 --> 00:48:53.230
LEIGH: Constanze, looking at the watch, I
think what we'll do is take a series of questions

00:48:53.230 --> 00:48:58.300
or comments, and then I'll ask you response
to any of them that you might wish to in the

00:48:58.300 --> 00:48:59.300
final remarks.

00:48:59.300 --> 00:49:00.300
Yes, please.

00:49:00.300 --> 00:49:01.300
There on the end.

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:02.300
Q: Hi.

00:49:02.300 --> 00:49:04.170
Adina Renee Adler with Alcoa.

00:49:04.170 --> 00:49:09.150
We're a company that has 1,800 employees at
14 locations around the U.K., which doesn't

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:17.460
sound like a lot of employees but it's a high-value,
highly automated industry that supplies to

00:49:17.460 --> 00:49:19.100
the automotive and the aerospace sectors.

00:49:19.100 --> 00:49:26.080
And of course, it was this model that was
built on developed on U.K. being a part of

00:49:26.080 --> 00:49:27.080
the EU.

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:34.752
But you know, a lot of the arguments for the
leave campaign, of course, is that those that

00:49:34.752 --> 00:49:42.520
support remain are of the elite and the industries
and, you know, those that may not understand

00:49:42.520 --> 00:49:43.640
what's going on at the micro level.

00:49:43.640 --> 00:49:48.080
So can I take you guys back to the micro level,
maybe the children in your ongoing divorce

00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:52.770
scenario, because those 1,800 employees are
also voters.

00:49:52.770 --> 00:49:58.010
And so our CEO, like many CEOs, has sent letters
to the employees saying, hey, look, as a company,

00:49:58.010 --> 00:50:01.370
we support remain because that's the foundation
of our business model.

00:50:01.370 --> 00:50:03.700
But it is ultimately your vote.

00:50:03.700 --> 00:50:08.860
So, you know, I don't know if it's putting
yourself in the shoes of a politician-I mean,

00:50:08.860 --> 00:50:14.610
how do you bring those arguments back into
the micro level, you know, in support of remain?

00:50:14.610 --> 00:50:19.200
You know, if it's an optimistic scenario or
if it's a pessimistic scenario, but how do

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:24.220
you tell people at the local level-like, what's
really driving those that are supporting leave?

00:50:24.220 --> 00:50:28.782
And you talk about immigration, but, like,
how do you come back and say, OK, we get that

00:50:28.782 --> 00:50:30.650
here's what the-
LEIGH: Can I ask you to keep-I'm sorry-

00:50:30.650 --> 00:50:33.130
Q: --the basic impact is?

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:35.910
I just-I need reality rather than just sort
of high level.

00:50:35.910 --> 00:50:36.910
LEIGH: Thanks very much.

00:50:36.910 --> 00:50:39.660
So the base, and what is the impact of that.

00:50:39.660 --> 00:50:40.660
Yes, please.

00:50:40.660 --> 00:50:42.020
Q: Thank you.

00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:44.850
Sonia Schott with Diario las Americas.

00:50:44.850 --> 00:50:52.330
I would like to know what are the implications
in terms of security-world security if the

00:50:52.330 --> 00:50:53.750
Brexit happens?

00:50:53.750 --> 00:50:55.180
Thank you.

00:50:55.180 --> 00:50:59.450
LEIGH: Any last comments or questions?

00:50:59.450 --> 00:51:00.450
There.

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:03.740
Q: What will Mr. Putin say the day after the
vote?

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:04.830
LEIGH: OK.

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:08.240
Thank you very much for those comments and
questions.

00:51:08.240 --> 00:51:10.180
Let's just do a final round.

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:14.650
And please respond to these questions and,
indeed, any other points that you would like

00:51:14.650 --> 00:51:17.750
to in the final wrap-up remarks.

00:51:17.750 --> 00:51:18.750
Constanze.

00:51:18.750 --> 00:51:21.890
STELZENM�LLER: I mean, the Kremlin and a
lot of a Russian politicians have already

00:51:21.890 --> 00:51:25.060
made it very clear where their sympathies
lie.

00:51:25.060 --> 00:51:30.440
I think you can go on Russian Twitter feeds-English-language
Twitter feeds anytime and see that.

00:51:30.440 --> 00:51:41.690
Which, you know, I'd be quite-that would give
me pause for thought if I were British, frankly.

00:51:41.690 --> 00:51:49.770
As for security, I think that Britain would
find itself diminished rather than strengthened

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:52.200
by Brexit.

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:57.050
One of the-I've watched quite closely successive
British strategic defense reviews.

00:51:57.050 --> 00:52:04.040
And the reality is that it's been about a
decade since Britain was able to afford and

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:09.390
deploy full-spectrum forces in the world way
that the nation-state used to think, you know,

00:52:09.390 --> 00:52:11.650
it was-that this due to itself.

00:52:11.650 --> 00:52:14.530
That's just no longer possible.

00:52:14.530 --> 00:52:19.380
And in fact, I think British policymakers
have glossed over the degree to which they

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:22.770
are deeply integrated with Europe on this
level as well.

00:52:22.770 --> 00:52:28.350
And one of the ways in which that has manifested
itself is that increasingly British security

00:52:28.350 --> 00:52:32.620
has done what IBM did a generation ago, it's
gone out of hardware and into software.

00:52:32.620 --> 00:52:38.110
In other words, it's emphasized the intelligence
relationship and it's deemphasized the hardware

00:52:38.110 --> 00:52:39.110
relationship.

00:52:39.110 --> 00:52:47.410
So Britain leaving, I wonder why Britain and
NATO should defend a Europe that it considers

00:52:47.410 --> 00:52:49.540
feckless in the context of the European Union.

00:52:49.540 --> 00:52:57.720
I don't get why Britain would want to defend
a Europe against a Russian threat if the Kremlin

00:52:57.720 --> 00:52:59.290
is espousing Brexit.

00:52:59.290 --> 00:53:02.620
These are things that worry me deeply.

00:53:02.620 --> 00:53:05.650
And I'll leave the Alcoa question to my colleagues.

00:53:05.650 --> 00:53:09.180
LEIGH: And the question about the micro impact?

00:53:09.180 --> 00:53:14.440
The EU is very often seen as a top-down project,
but what would people at the factory floor

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:18.110
level or those at the more popular level,
what would be the effect for them?

00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:21.510
POSEN: It's unfortunate our colleague from
Alcoa chose to phrase it as I don't need high

00:53:21.510 --> 00:53:31.230
level I need micro because that means one
of two things: It means either that-and this

00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:38.600
may be the reality-one needs a set of attractive
lies to appeal to people who are nativist

00:53:38.600 --> 00:53:50.110
and who are making their decision on completely
bogus basis, or it means that Alcoa and other

00:53:50.110 --> 00:53:53.070
business leaders are not being frank enough.

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:58.000
Because the reality is-I can't obviously speak
for your company-but the auto industry will

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:03.490
essentially shut down in the U.K. if it's
not an export model to Europe-if they're not

00:54:03.490 --> 00:54:08.320
part of the single market, if they're not
part of the same regulations.

00:54:08.320 --> 00:54:13.560
It's always been a lot cheaper to move to
Eastern Europe, to move to Southern Europe.

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:18.850
High value added, you know, it's not that
hard to find high value added skilled workers

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:24.060
of manufacturers throughout Europe.

00:54:24.060 --> 00:54:25.530
So it's hard.

00:54:25.530 --> 00:54:32.731
And then if we talk about a pound that declines
by, say, I don't know, 10, 20 percent, a Britain

00:54:32.731 --> 00:54:44.080
that imports vast quantities of food, clothing,
entertainment, everything except fuel, that's

00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:47.980
a huge hit to the people's standard of living.

00:54:47.980 --> 00:54:54.680
NHS, as people have pointed out who have done
the real numbers, the migrants, because they're

00:54:54.680 --> 00:54:58.490
younger and hardworking are essentially net
payers into NHS.

00:54:58.490 --> 00:55:05.970
And as I mentioned, there is more than a million-because
it's not just Spain-closer to 2 million older

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:12.280
Brits living in Europe whose healthcare needs
are being taken care of by European health

00:55:12.280 --> 00:55:13.930
systems.

00:55:13.930 --> 00:55:17.600
And they are going to have to come home or
the U.K. is going to have to start paying

00:55:17.600 --> 00:55:19.900
those foreign health systems for the cost.

00:55:19.900 --> 00:55:23.450
I mean, it's a huge material hit to the average
working people.

00:55:23.450 --> 00:55:28.950
I know you didn't mean it that way, but I'm
just-I'm just-it is ultimately the same question

00:55:28.950 --> 00:55:31.690
as with Trump, as with Marine Le Pen.

00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:36.580
The InFacts campaign that Sebastian, I think,
has been part of; my friend Jonathan Portes,

00:55:36.580 --> 00:55:42.470
who's been out there doing this-I mean, at
some point, whether it's journalists or politicians

00:55:42.470 --> 00:55:51.000
or us in the supposed elites-there is a problem
when you say these are the facts, and the

00:55:51.000 --> 00:55:57.980
people who stand up and lie can't be contradicted
in an effective way.

00:55:57.980 --> 00:56:02.621
So the stuff they say about migration, for
example, and I'll end here-and, again, I know

00:56:02.621 --> 00:56:06.830
you know this, but I don't know this isn't
micro.

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:08.540
You're living in the middle of England.

00:56:08.540 --> 00:56:13.070
And you say, I really am sick of having a
Polish deli in this town and a group of people

00:56:13.070 --> 00:56:16.440
who don't look like me and speak and I think
it's bad.

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:17.440
Fine.

00:56:17.440 --> 00:56:19.400
So you pass this law.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:23.070
You put up more immigration barriers.

00:56:23.070 --> 00:56:32.020
What's going to happen is you then still are
going to have huge pressures for illegal immigration,

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:36.180
because the French are just going to stop
policing the border at Calais.

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:40.880
The Irish are going to be not very happy,
and the Danes, and all the people coastal

00:56:40.880 --> 00:56:43.060
to the U.K.

00:56:43.060 --> 00:56:49.020
You have all the non-EU migration that's been
coming for decades, and is much bigger than

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:52.460
the intra-EU migration, because the intra-EU
migration people go home.

00:56:52.460 --> 00:56:55.810
During the recession, a lot of the Poles went
home to Poland.

00:56:55.810 --> 00:56:57.800
It's just not going to work.

00:56:57.800 --> 00:57:06.821
The only way it works is if you do truly get
intrusive in draconian ways in the U.K. and

00:57:06.821 --> 00:57:08.280
change huge numbers of laws.

00:57:08.280 --> 00:57:12.270
I mean, again, it's like the reduction ad
absurdum of Trump's wall.

00:57:12.270 --> 00:57:16.070
You know, you can literally deport millions
of people.

00:57:16.070 --> 00:57:19.600
You can literally build walls.

00:57:19.600 --> 00:57:21.710
But it destroys your society.

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:24.280
And so in the end, I don't view that as high
level.

00:57:24.280 --> 00:57:26.810
I view that as very real.

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:31.330
And it is, of course, and I completely sympathize
with you, and I feel unable to sleep at night

00:57:31.330 --> 00:57:40.580
whether it's here or in Brexit, that things
that are commonsense realities somehow cannot

00:57:40.580 --> 00:57:41.600
be communicated.

00:57:41.600 --> 00:57:45.830
And maybe it's because people of us, like
at CFR are inherently bad messengers.

00:57:45.830 --> 00:57:51.010
But somebody has to be able to say the other
stuff is a lie.

00:57:51.010 --> 00:57:52.010
LEIGH: Hmm.

00:57:52.010 --> 00:57:53.010
Sebastian, briefly.

00:57:53.010 --> 00:57:54.440
MALLABY: Well, just briefly.

00:57:54.440 --> 00:57:58.410
So, I mean, I want to reiterate what Adam
just said.

00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:03.360
It does annoy me deeply when Michael Grove,
one of the principal leave campaigners says,

00:58:03.360 --> 00:58:06.790
quote: The country is fed up with experts.

00:58:06.790 --> 00:58:09.180
I mean, what do we prefer, non-experts?

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:11.424
We prefer people who don't know what they're
talking about to people who do?

00:58:11.424 --> 00:58:16.300
I mean, at a certain point a lie is a lie
and something which is wrong is wrong.

00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:19.270
And we shouldn't say, oh, but people don't
want to hear that.

00:58:19.270 --> 00:58:23.920
No, we need to find a way of actually communicating
the truth, because the truth matters.

00:58:23.920 --> 00:58:31.110
And I mean, I'm drive to this, and this organization
I'm part of, InFacts.org, it's about the facts

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:32.110
for being in.

00:58:32.110 --> 00:58:34.650
And there are so many untruths on the other
side.

00:58:34.650 --> 00:58:39.360
When they go around in their campaign bus,
which has on the side that we contribute 350

00:58:39.360 --> 00:58:45.120
million pounds a week to Brussels, when the
real number isn't 350 million, and they know

00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:47.610
it, and the national statistician has said
it twice.

00:58:47.610 --> 00:58:49.970
And everybody said it's wrong.

00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:52.520
And so they interviewed the guys on the bus
and they say: How do you feel about going

00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:56.340
around in bus which says something which is
not true on the side?

00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:58.260
And the answer is: I'm delirious.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:58:59.260
That's Boris Johnson.

00:58:59.260 --> 00:59:00.820
He's a classicist, very educated.

00:59:00.820 --> 00:59:02.680
So he chooses words carefully.

00:59:02.680 --> 00:59:09.010
Delirious can mean very happy or it can mean
I'm sort of, you know, so tired I'm hallucinating.

00:59:09.010 --> 00:59:11.030
And he knows that double entendre.

00:59:11.030 --> 00:59:14.280
And he's basically saying: To heck with the
facts.

00:59:14.280 --> 00:59:15.740
I don't think we should give into that.

00:59:15.740 --> 00:59:17.750
But let me just leave you with one last thought.

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:23.530
So I think it's true that when you communicate
with people just hard facts, you know, I believe

00:59:23.530 --> 00:59:26.290
they're important, I don't think we should
give up on them.

00:59:26.290 --> 00:59:27.290
But you might need something else.

00:59:27.290 --> 00:59:32.090
And I think the something else is best epitomized
by the video that went viral recently where

00:59:32.090 --> 00:59:36.882
the former Prime Minister Gordon Brown is
walking around the rooms of Coventry Cathedral,

00:59:36.882 --> 00:59:40.515
symbol of the destruction wrought by the division
of Europe in the Second World War, and he

00:59:40.515 --> 00:59:41.515
appeals to people.

00:59:41.515 --> 00:59:46.790
He looks in the camera and he says: We have
now a Europe which is not about armaments,

00:59:46.790 --> 00:59:48.500
it's about arguments.

00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:51.410
We have left the blood and the division behind.

00:59:51.410 --> 00:59:52.740
Do you want to change that?

00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:58.400
And I think there is something big about history
and overcoming the terrible history of Europe,

00:59:58.400 --> 01:00:02.960
where very century people destroyed cities
and killed each other.

01:00:02.960 --> 01:00:08.440
And I think that, at an emotional level, is
what you need to counter the leave campaign

01:00:08.440 --> 01:00:13.570
stuff about sovereignty and take control,
which is ultimately facile and ahistorical.

01:00:13.570 --> 01:00:18.470
LEIGH: And to leave you with another slightly
positive thought, if despite expectations

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:25.140
there were to be, in response to your question,
a decent majority in favor of remain, then

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:29.860
one of the things that would happen would
be the implementation of the concessions that

01:00:29.860 --> 01:00:36.890
Cameron won, of which is more emphasis on
competitiveness, better regulation, getting

01:00:36.890 --> 01:00:39.750
rid of unnecessary red tape, and so on.

01:00:39.750 --> 01:00:46.730
So it's just conceivable that the U.K. might
then engage a little more on a positive agenda,

01:00:46.730 --> 01:00:51.180
and might be a little bit less cautious also
on the diplomatic and security front when

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:56.100
it comes to things like the Normandy formula
and so on, from which they've been studiously

01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.140
absent for some years.

01:00:58.140 --> 01:00:59.690
I'd like to thank our three panelists very
much.

01:00:59.690 --> 01:01:00.690
Thank you for being here.

01:01:00.690 --> 01:01:01.690
(Applause.)

01:01:01.690 --> 01:01:01.691
(END)

